# Answer key for B2 t5_metamorphic.sql - pairs and one realistic model.
#
# WRITTEN FROM SOURCE BEFORE THE ANALYSER WAS RUN OVER THIS FILE.
#
# P1-P4 must be equal: their facts are listed once, at spelling A.
# P5-P8 are SEAM pairs: the same rows, labelled differently by today's conventions. The
# key holds the UNION of both spellings' facts, each labelled by the rule its own
# spelling falls under, and says which spelling owns which. The pair check is expected to
# report them as DIFFER; that difference is the finding, put up for decision.

@ 24 P1 EXISTS vs IN
value staging.customers.id -> mart.top_customers.customer_id identity
value staging.customers.segment -> mart.top_customers.region identity
filter staging.orders.customer_id, staging.customers.id, staging.orders.status -> mart.top_customers

@ 45 P2 IN vs = ANY
value staging.orders.id -> mart.channel_mix.orders identity
value staging.orders.channel -> mart.channel_mix.channel identity
filter staging.orders.id, staging.payments.order_id, staging.payments.method -> mart.channel_mix

@ 61 P3 CTE vs inlined derived table
value core.dim_store.region, core.dim_warehouse.region -> mart.kpi_rollup.region identity
filter core.dim_warehouse.status -> mart.kpi_rollup

@ 83 P4 UNION vs UNION of DISTINCTs
value staging.orders.channel, staging.payments.method -> mart.sales_daily.region identity

@ 100 P5 SEAM semi-join: IN vs JOIN + DISTINCT
value core.dim_product.product_key -> mart.product_performance.product_id identity
value core.dim_product.category_name -> mart.product_performance.category_name identity
filter core.fct_order_line.quantity -> mart.product_performance
filter core.dim_product.product_key, core.fct_order_line.product_key -> mart.product_performance
note Spelling A only: the IN's match columns are filters. In B they are a join key, structural under D-5. DECIDED 2026-10-07 (D-52): a semi-join's key is a filter, and this divergence is by design.

@ 122 P6 SEAM anti-join: EXCEPT vs NOT EXISTS
value staging.customers.id -> mart.customer_summary.customer_id identity
filter staging.orders.customer_id, staging.orders.status -> mart.customer_summary
@ 128 P6 B (spelling B's own facts, at its line - B4-04)
filter staging.customers.id -> mart.customer_summary
note Spelling B only: the correlation's outer operand. In A, customers.id is the first arm and supplies only a value (convention (a)). DECIDED 2026-10-07 (D-52): an anti-join's key is a filter; the EXCEPT spelling differs by design.

@ 143 P7 SEAM scalar subquery vs LEFT JOIN LATERAL
value core.dim_carrier.id -> mart.carrier_scorecard.carrier_id identity
value core.fct_shipment.transit_days -> mart.carrier_scorecard.avg_transit_days aggregated
# D-58: core.fct_shipment.carrier_id, core.dim_carrier.id matched the aggregated lookup's rows to mart.carrier_scorecard.avg_transit_days's - influence, as a GROUP BY is (was a value).
influence core.fct_shipment.carrier_id, core.dim_carrier.id -> mart.carrier_scorecard.avg_transit_days
note Spelling A only (D-12).
note RELABELLED UNDER D-14: spelling B's LATERAL cannot remove a carrier, so its WHERE is the same value as A's (D-12). P7 is now a MUST-EQUAL pair.

@ 166 P8 SEAM ordered pick vs LEFT JOIN to DISTINCT ON
value staging.orders.id -> mart.sales_daily.orders identity
value staging.payments.amount -> mart.sales_daily.net_amount identity
# D-58: staging.payments.order_id, staging.orders.id -> mart.sales_daily.net_amount is a lookup key (join_key, not scored; was a conditional value).
influence staging.payments.paid_at -> mart.sales_daily.net_amount
note Spelling A only (D-12, D-13).
@ 176 P8 B (spelling B's own facts, at its line - B4-04)
influence staging.payments.order_id -> mart.sales_daily.net_amount
note RELABELLED UNDER D-14: under the LEFT JOIN the DISTINCT ON removes no order, so its key and tie-breaker decide WHICH amount arrives - influence, as D-13 says of the ordered pick. paid_at now agrees across the pair. payments.order_id still differs (influence in B, a conditional value in A): that is the match-key seam of P5/P6, decided as D-52 (2026-10-07): a divergence by design.

@ 196 R1 the monthly carrier scorecard
value core.fct_shipment.carrier_id -> mart.carrier_scorecard.carrier_id identity
value core.dim_carrier.name -> mart.carrier_scorecard.carrier_name identity
# D-58: core.dim_carrier.id, core.fct_shipment.carrier_id -> mart.carrier_scorecard.carrier_name is a lookup key (join_key, not scored; was a conditional value).
value core.fct_shipment.shipped_date -> mart.carrier_scorecard.period_month derived
value core.fct_shipment.delivered_date, core.fct_shipment.promised_date -> mart.carrier_scorecard.on_time_pct aggregated
value core.fct_shipment.cost, core.fct_shipment.weight_kg -> mart.carrier_scorecard.cost_per_kg aggregated
influence core.fct_shipment.carrier_id, core.fct_shipment.shipped_date -> mart.carrier_scorecard.shipments, mart.carrier_scorecard.on_time_pct, mart.carrier_scorecard.cost_per_kg
note `shipments` is COUNT(*): no value source, but aggregated, so the grouping reaches it.
filter core.fct_shipment.status, core.fct_payment.order_id, core.fct_shipment.order_id, core.fct_payment.status -> mart.carrier_scorecard
filter staging.shipments.order_id, staging.shipments.status, staging.returns.order_line_id, staging.returns.reason_code -> mart.carrier_scorecard
note `WHERE mo.n >= 10` tests COUNT(*), which has no column to name.
